===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The condition of not being [[threaten]]ed, especially physically, psychologically, emotionally, or financially.
#: {{ux|en|Chad always carries a gun for his own '''security'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|The pink blanket gives little Mary lots of '''security'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Antony and Cleopatra|III|vii|passage=Give up yourself merely to chance and hazard, / From firm '''security'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Swift Conduct of the Allies|pages=73–74|pageref=74|passage=Was there no way to provide for the Safety of ''Britain'', or the '''Security''' for its Trade, but by the ''French'' Kings turning his own Arms to beat his Grandſon out of ''Spain''?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1918|author=James George Frazer|title=Folk-Lore In The Old Testament|volume=1|page=51|text=An earthly paradise, where, free from care and toil, they could live on the sweet fruits of a delightful garden, and where birds and beasts frisked about them in fearless '''security'''.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} Something that secures.
# An [[organization]] or [[department]] responsible for providing security by enforcing [[laws]], [[rules]], and [[regulations]] as well as maintaining [[order]].
#: {{ux|en|Pamela works in '''security'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|Leave right now or I'll call '''security'''.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1977|artist=w:Crosby, Stills & Nash|author=w:Graham Nash|album={{w|CSN (album)|CSN}}|title=w:Just a Song Before I Go|text={{...|She helped me with my suitcase / She stands  before my eyes / Driving me to the airport / And to the friendly skies}} Going through '''security''' I held her for so long / She finally looked at me in love and she was gone}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2012-12-14|author=w:Simon Jenkins| volume=188| issue=2| page=23| magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly| title=We mustn't overreact to North Korea boys' toys|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/commentisfree/2012/dec/14/north-korea-boys-toys-threat-reaction| passage=The threat of terrorism to the British lies in the overreaction to it of British governments. Each one in turn clicks up the ratchet of surveillance, intrusion and '''security'''. Each one diminishes liberty.}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-06-08|volume=407|issue=8839|page=52| title=The new masters and commanders|url=http://www.economist.com/news/international/21579039-chinas-growing-empire-ports-abroad-mainly-about-trade-not-aggression-new-masters| passage=From the ground, Colombo’s port does not look like much. Those entering it are greeted by wire fences, walls dating back to colonial times and '''security''' posts. For mariners leaving the port after lonely nights on the high seas, the delights of the B52 Night Club and Stallion Pub lie a stumble away.}}
# {{lb|en|legal}} Something that secures the fulfillment of an obligation or law.
# {{lb|en|legal}} Freedom from [[apprehension]].
# {{senseid|en|Q169489}} {{lb|en|finance|often|in plural}} A [[tradeable]] [[financial asset]], such as a share of [[stock]] or any of various classes of [[bond#English:_Q11693|bond]].
#: {{hyper|en|financial asset<id:Q2823610>|<|asset<id:Q46737>}}
# {{lb|en|finance}} Proof of ownership of [[stock]]s, [[bond]]s or other [[investment]] instruments.
# {{lb|en|finance}} [[property|Property]] etc. temporarily [[relinquish]]ed to [[guarantee]] repayment of a [[loan]].
#: {{nearsyn|en|collateral#Noun}}
# A [[guarantee]].
#* {{RQ:Swift Gulliver|part=I|pages=106–107|pageref=106|passage=As to Perſons of Quality, they give '''Security''' to appropriate a certain Sum for each Child, ſuitable to their Condition; and theſe Funds are always managed with good Husbandry and the moſt exact Juſtice.}}
#* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|chapter=12|passage=Those who lent him money lent it on no '''security''' but his bare word.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[carelessness|Carelessness]]; [[negligence]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2|III|ii|passage=He means, my lord, that we are too remiss,<br>Whilst Bolingbroke, through our '''security''',<br>Grows strong and great in substance and in power.}}

